"""
FreeCAD AI Bridge - Industrial Grade Edition
Author: Claude AI Assistant (Hardened by Security Audit) + Imagineer
Version: 3.4.6 - Imagineering Edition (Ollama / Local AI)
Date: 2026-11-01

CHANGES IN v3.4.6 (2026-11-01) - ONE BAD COMMAND NO LONGER WEDGES THE BRIDGE:
======================================
Observed live on FreeCAD 26.3: the reader sat 2035 bytes behind with
last_position frozen at 372, commands_processed 0 and errors_count 0 - while
monitoring_active stayed true and the heartbeat kept ticking every few
seconds. Every health signal said the bridge was fine. It answered nothing,
including a plain 'hello'.

Two cooperating defects caused that, and neither reported itself:

  1. The outer handler in process_ai_commands() deliberately left
     _state.last_position alone on a fatal error ("Don't update position on
     fatal errors"). The offending bytes therefore stayed at the head of the
     queue forever.
  2. The mtime gate committed _state._last_input_mtime BEFORE the file had
     been read successfully. So after a failure, this revision of the input
     was already marked "seen": later polls returned early, and when a new
     write finally bumped the mtime, the reader seeked back to the same
     frozen position and hit the same bad bytes again.

Together: a single unprocessable command permanently silenced the channel
with every status field still reading healthy.

  - The fatal path now skips past the bytes it could not process, says how
    many it dropped, and sets error_state so the condition is visible.
    Dropping pending input is a real cost; being unreachable with a healthy
    heartbeat is a worse one.
  - The mtime is now committed only after a pass that actually completed.

ALSO: _ConsoleCapture.start() has never worked on any current build.
App.Console.AddObserver does not exist on FreeCAD 1.1 or 26.3 - verified,
dir(App.Console) has GetObservers and no AddObserver on both - so the bare
"except Exception: self._observer = None" has always been taken and stop()
has always returned empty lists. Every 'report_view' this bridge has ever
returned was silently empty. It now records WHY and reports
capture_unavailable, instead of handing back an empty capture that reads as
"the command produced no output".

NOT a bridge defect, recorded here so it is not re-chased: a run_macro that
failed with "name 'getString' is not defined" was a bug in the CALLED macro
plus a real bug in FreeCAD 26.3's bundled pivy - Mod/pivy/coin.py:754 has
"return getString().__iter__()" missing its "self.", so ANY iteration or
"x in name" test on an SbName raises NameError. Wrap getName() in str().
The bridge reported that error correctly.

CHANGES IN v3.4.5 (2026-08-23) - THE CONFIG NO LONGER FAILS SILENTLY:
======================================
get_ai_io_dir() and get_learning_dir() both read ai_bridge_config.json with a
bare open() - no encoding argument at all - and swallowed every failure into a
warning plus a fall-through to the default.

A BOM on that file, which PowerShell's Set-Content writes by DEFAULT, therefore
moved the operator's IO directory back to the default without the bridge
stopping or the operator being told anything that reads as an error. Everything
downstream then wrote to a channel nobody had chosen.

This is the same defect GIMP 0.1.0 shipped, where a BOM on its config silently
unwatched the remote inbox for hours while ping and info both answered healthy.
See HQ/41_AI_Bridge/10_Core/RESILIENCE_CONTRACT_0.0.01.md: repair what is
provably safe, fail loudly otherwise, never degrade to defaults.

Both loaders now go through resilience.load_json_repairing when 10_Core is
reachable. A BOM is repaired, damage needing a guess is refused loudly, and the
defaults are used only when the file genuinely does not exist. The import
degrades exactly the way the wire import above it does, for the same reason:
this macro runs inside FreeCAD and must never fail to load over a path problem.

MAJOR CHANGES IN v3.4.4 (2026-08-21) - WIRE 2 / AI REDUNDANCY:
======================================
Emits the shared wire-2 envelope defined in
HQ/41_AI_Bridge/10_Core/WIRE_CONTRACT_0.0.01.md, so this bridge can be driven
by any AI through an IDE rather than only by one vendor's assistant.

Why this mattered here more than anywhere else: measured across 549 real lines
of this bridge's outbox, NOT ONE carried an "id" at any level a generic reader
inspects. The correlation id lived at content.command_id, success was a bool at
content.success, and the payload was nested under "content". A language model
coped by reading the stdout and inferring which request a result answered. No
CLI, MCP server or local Ollama model can do that -- so in practice this bridge
was unusable by anything except a model clever enough to paper over it.

- write_output() and flush_result_buffer() now pass every entry through
  _conform(), which calls the shared wire.upgrade().
- The wire import is SOFT here, unlike the other five bridges which assert:
  this macro runs inside FreeCAD and must not be taken down by a path problem.
  It degrades to wire 1 plus client-side normalisation.
- No verb, GUI or sandbox behaviour changed.

MAJOR CHANGES IN v3.4.3 (2026-07-25) - RETRIEVAL FIX (PHASE 4 TUNING):
======================================
  * ROOT CAUSE FOUND: TechDataReviewer.search_knowledge() tests
    `query_lower in category.lower()` - it substring-matches the WHOLE query
    against category/key names. 'box' matches; 'create a 10mm box with a hole'
    matches NOTHING. v3.4.0-3.4.2 passed the raw user prompt straight in, so
    knowledge retrieval returned ZERO hits for every realistic request. The
    thin ~300-token context was not under-tuned, it was empty of retrieved
    knowledge. The KB was never consulted in practice.
  * extract_query_terms(): tokenises the prompt, drops stopwords (including
    'make'/'create', which match every constructor and rank nothing), and
    expands through a FreeCAD domain alias map - users say "hole", the KB
    indexes "cut"/"boolean".
  * retrieve_patterns(): searches PER TERM, merges, dedupes, and ranks by
    distinct term coverage (whole-word matches score double) instead of
    taking whatever came back first.
  * _format_hit(): compact one-line rendering replaces json.dumps(indent=1),
    which was burning 3-5x the tokens for identical information.
  * recent_successes() is now RELEVANCE-ranked, not pure recency - it took the
    last five verified commands regardless of whether they related to the
    request. Recency remains the fallback when nothing scores.
  * NEW "Context" button + describe_last_build(): shows the search terms used,
    hits found vs included, token split, and whether pattern selection fell
    back to recency. Rebuilds against whatever is in the prompt box, so
    retrieval can be tested WITHOUT spending a generation. A thin context is
    now diagnosable instead of mysterious.
  * stable_hash() updated for the new (text, report) return signature; it
    hashes the prompt-independent verified set so registration stays stable.

MAJOR CHANGES IN v3.4.2 (2026-07-25) - LOCAL-CHAT MESSAGING FIX:
======================================
  * BUG (mine, from 3.4.1): selecting Ollama and pressing Open Chat launched
    the panel correctly but then showed the EDITOR success dialog - "opened
    with AI Bridge Workspace / will automatically read .windsurfrules". Every
    line of that was false for Ollama. The rules_map lookup had no 'ollama'
    entry and fell through to a hardcoded '.windsurfrules' default.
  * open_ide_with_context() now branches on is_local_chat BEFORE doing any
    editor work: no IDE instruction file is generated, no workspace is synced,
    and the dialog states what actually happens - knowledge is INJECTED into
    the system prompt per message, with the live token count shown, and the
    model cannot browse files the way Claude Code can.
  * rules_map: 'windsurf' -> 'devin' ('.devin/', legacy .windsurfrules still
    honoured), 'claude-code' added, and the silent '.windsurfrules' fallback
    replaced with an honest generic string. That default was the root cause.

MAJOR CHANGES IN v3.4.1 (2026-07-25) - NAMING + GUI PLACEMENT + DEVIN FIX:
======================================
  [NAMING CORRECTION]
  * "Hermes" is GONE. It was a poor choice: Hermes is a MODEL that runs
    inside Ollama (NousResearch Hermes), so using it as the name of the
    panel that connects TO Ollama implied you were running Hermes when you
    were actually running qwen3-coder. Everything is now named for what it
    is: Ollama. agent_id changed from "hermes" to "ollama".
    NOTE: learning-database entries tagged source_agent "hermes" from a
    v3.4.0 session keep that tag and stay unverified - harmless, but they
    will show as a separate agent in get_provenance_stats().

  [GUI PLACEMENT]
  * The standalone "Ollama" button is removed. Ollama is now an entry in
    the existing IDE dropdown, so ONE selector answers ONE question:
    where does my AI come from? Selecting it changes the launch button
    from "Open IDE" to "Open Chat" and opens the local chat panel.
    Ollama is not an editor - there is no application to launch - so it is
    routed inside launch_ide() before the install-detection check.

  [DEVIN DESKTOP - EXTERNAL BREAKING CHANGE, NOT OURS]
  * Cognition rebranded Windsurf to Devin Desktop on 2026-06-02 via an OTA
    update; the Windows binary is now Devin.exe. The old 'windsurf' CLI
    probe therefore reported "Not Found" on any machine that had simply
    updated. The 'windsurf' entry is replaced by 'devin', detection gained
    a cli_alt fallback list so legacy installs still resolve, and a saved
    preference of 'windsurf' migrates silently to 'devin'.

MAJOR CHANGES IN v3.4.0 (2026-07-25) - OLLAMA INTEGRATION, PHASES 1-10:
======================================
  SCOPE: This release lands the *foundation* only - Config, provider-agnostic
  LLM client, threaded worker, and a minimal chat panel. It deliberately does
  NOT submit anything to the bridge yet (that is Phase 5). The goal of this
  build is to prove that a 20-60 second local model generation CANNOT stall
  the FreeCAD GUI. Everything here is additive; set
  Config.OLLAMA_ENABLED = False to disable the entire feature path and get
  v3.3.3 behaviour back.

  [PHASE 1 - CONFIG]
  * New OLLAMA_* block in Config. num_ctx is set EXPLICITLY -
    Ollama defaults to 4096 and truncates SILENTLY, which would eat the
    injected knowledge base without any warning in the logs.

  [PHASE 2 - LLMClient]
  * Provider-agnostic client (Ollama local + OpenRouter cloud) over stdlib
    urllib - no new dependencies inside FreeCAD's Python.
  * Streaming for both wire formats: Ollama NDJSON (/api/chat) and
    OpenAI-style SSE (/v1/chat/completions).
  * ensure_server_running() starts `ollama serve` on demand so Ollama does
    not need to autostart and squat on RAM.

  [PHASE 3 - THREADING (the whole point of this release)]
  * OllamaWorker(QThread): network I/O ONLY. It never touches App, Gui,
    the document, or App.Console - FreeCAD's API is not thread-safe and this
    boundary is absolute. Results cross back via Qt signals, which Qt queues
    onto the GUI thread for us.
  * _ollama_generating global is separate from _executing_command so the
    v3.3.3 watchdog does not misreport a long generation as a GUI freeze.
  * OllamaChatDialog carries a LIVE HEARTBEAT (100ms QTimer spinner). If the
    GUI thread ever blocks, the spinner visibly stops - that is the
    acceptance test for this phase, not a benchmark score.

  [DELIBERATELY NOT IN THIS BUILD]
  * No bridge submission, no code extraction, no auto-repair loop, no
    provenance tagging, no registration handshake. Phases 4-10.

MAJOR CHANGES IN v3.3.3 (2026-07-12) - GUI-FREEZE HARDENING:
======================================
  * Root-caused a hard GUI lockup: Bridge commands execute synchronously on the
    FreeCAD GUI thread, so any long op (heavy Coin3D shadow render, big recompute,
    infinite loop) froze the whole app with no watchdog. FreeCAD's API is not
    thread-safe, so we CANNOT move exec off the GUI thread; instead:
  * Removed all time.sleep() calls from the GUI thread (file-lock retry + 50ms
    batch delay) - locked input file no longer micro-stalls the UI.
  * Added a background daemon WATCHDOG thread that keeps running while the GUI is
    blocked (GIL releases during OCCT calls). It observes _executing_command and,
    if a command holds the GUI thread past Config.WATCHDOG_WARN_SEC, writes a
    diagnostic to the error log + ai_watchdog.json naming the freeze. Philosophy
    (Rule 42): DETECT, WARN, NEVER BLOCK. It cannot kill a frozen command, but a
    freeze is now logged instead of a silent mystery. COMMAND_TIMEOUT_SEC repurposed.

